<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fossa Carolina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/793">793</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Karlsgraben</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fossa Carolina was a canal that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> ordered dug in 793 near Treuchtlingen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> to link the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> river systems through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmuhl">Altmühl</a> and the Rezat. The work was abandoned unfinished, apparently because of rain and soft ground, but a stretch of the ditch survives as a pond and earthworks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fossa-carolina.jpg" alt="Fossa Carolina" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fossa_Carolina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Near Treuchtlingen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenburg-in-bayern">Weißenburg in Bayern</a> the European watershed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> basins is very narrow, with only about 2 km of fairly level ground between the Swabian Rezat and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmuhl">Altmühl</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einhard">Einhard</a>, writing some two decades later, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> was persuaded by supposed experts that ships could then travel in comfort from the Rhine to the Danube. In the autumn of 793 he assembled a large workforce and supervised in person the digging of a moat 2,000 steps long and 300 feet wide.</p><p>The work seems to have been meant to ease the carriage of goods between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>; another theory holds that its chief purpose was to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s war vessels back from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. In the 19th century the project inspired King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Ludwig I of Bavaria</a> to build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-canal">Ludwig Canal</a>, which opened in 1846, and the Rhine–Main–Danube Canal later crossed the same watershed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A stretch of about 500 m in Graben, part of the municipality of Treuchtlingen, still holds water. Heaps of excavated earth marking the banks run for around 1.2 km in all and rise in places to 10 m, and a waterless section of 700 m to the north remains visible in the landscape, although its earthworks are almost levelled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Research by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-koch">Robert Koch</a> in 1992/3 showed that the floor of the canal was not level but rose in steps from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmuhl">Altmühl</a> towards the north. Geoarchaeological work in 2009 found that a dam at Dettenheim, once thought early medieval, was probably built only in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and did not serve to dam the Rezat. A research project of the DFG begun in 2012, part of a study of ports from imperial Roman times to the early Middle Ages, examines what the original canal may have looked like.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fossa Carolina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Carolingian royal estate stood there, and Charlemagne's attempted canal between the Rhine and Danube basins, the <b>Fossa Carolina</b>, lay nearby.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-weissenburg">Free Imperial City of Weissenburg</a></li></ul></section>
